.\" Hand-written. The event list and the ABI number must match
.\" mc_common::plugin — a tier-1 test asserts they do.
.TH MC-PLUGINS 5 "" "mc" "mc manual"
.SH NAME
mc-plugins \- plugin manifest format for mc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/*.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
A plugin is a
.B .deb
that drops a TOML manifest into
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/
and an executable into
.IR /usr/libexec/mc/ .
.BR mc (1)
reads every manifest in that directory at startup, in filename order, and
invokes the executable across a process boundary.
.PP
Out of process, rather than a shared library, because Rust has no stable ABI: a
.BR dlopen -based
plugin would have to be rebuilt in lockstep with core, which defeats the point
of "adding a capability is installing another package". One fork and exec per
invocation is nothing at command-line latency.
.PP
A manifest that fails to load is reported, never silently skipped \(em
.B mc plugins
prints the problem. A plugin whose command was expected and has vanished is
exactly the case a silent skip would hide.
.SH FIELDS
.TP
.B abi
.RI ( "integer, required" )
The plugin interface version this plugin was built against. Core implements
.BR 1 .
A manifest declaring anything else is refused by name, and its commands and
hooks do not run.
.IP
This replaces a versioned
.BR Depends: .
The arrangement it replaced had a plugin's postinst source another package's
private shell library, so a missed version-floor bump left dpkg configuring the
plugin against a library without the function it called. Core reads the number
instead.
.TP
.B name
.RI ( "string, required" )
The plugin's name. Also the naming rule for its manual page:
.B mc man <command>
looks for
.BI mc- name (1),
so a plugin named
.B backup
must ship
.BR mc-backup (1).
.TP
.B bin
.RI ( "path, required" )
The executable. Conventionally
.IR /usr/libexec/mc/mc-<name> ,
which is deliberately not on
.BR PATH :
it is invoked by core and is not a command surface anyone should script
against.
.SH "[[commands]]"
One table per subcommand contributed. Only a registered name is dispatchable \(em
resolving to some executable is not sufficient, or an internal entry point
would become callable from the command line, skipping the guards, the lock and
the configuration loading its real entry point performs.
.TP
.B name
The subcommand as typed:
.B name = "backup"
makes
.B mc backup
work.
.TP
.B about
One-line description, shown by
.BR "mc plugins" .
.PP
Core invokes it as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.RI < bin "> command <" name "> [args...]"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The plugin enforces its own privilege guard. Core does not refuse on its behalf:
only the plugin knows whether the subcommand reads or writes.
.SH "[[hooks]]"
One table per lifecycle event.
.TP
.B event
One of:
.RS
.TP
.B pre-start
Before the unit is started.
.TP
.B pre-stop
Before the server is told to stop.
.TP
.B pre-backup
Before a backup archives the world.
.TP
.B post-backup
After a backup, whether or not it succeeded.
.TP
.B post-install
After a server is installed.
.TP
.B post-upgrade
After a server is upgraded.
.RE
.IP
An unrecognised event is a manifest error, not a silent no-op: a hook declared
as
.B pre-stopp
would otherwise install cleanly and never run.
.TP
.B fatal
.RI ( "boolean, default " false )
Whether a non-zero exit aborts the operation around it. Contributing a step
must not become a way to break the core path, so this is off unless asked for.
.IP
.B pre-stop
and
.B post-backup
may never be fatal, and a manifest that says otherwise is refused at load time.
A shutdown does not stop because a warning could not be delivered, and a
backup's save-on must run even after the archive failed \(em a live server left
with saving disabled loses everything since the last flush.
.PP
Core invokes a hook as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.RI < bin "> hook <" event ">"
.fi
.RE
.PP
with a JSON payload on standard input. Every hook registered for an event runs,
in filename order, even when an earlier one failed: they are independent
contributions, not a pipeline.
.SS "Re-entrancy"
A hook may run
.BR mc ,
and that re-entry dispatches hooks of its own. Nothing inside a single process
can see a chain that has already crossed a process boundary, so core carries the
nesting state in the environment \(em
.B MC_HOOK_DEPTH
and
.B MC_HOOK_CHAIN
\(em which every descendant inherits.
.PP
Core refuses to dispatch a hook when either bound is reached:
.TP
.I a cycle
The same
.IB plugin : event
pair is already active further up the chain. This is the shorter and likelier
failure: two plugins whose hooks trigger each other's events ping-pong forever
at a depth that never reaches the limit.
.TP
.I depth
Dispatch is already
.B 2
levels deep. One level is the normal case and two covers a hook that
legitimately drives a core operation with hooks of its own \(em a
.B post-install
that takes a backup. Deeper is a plugin calling back into the operation that
invoked it, which does not terminate on its own.
.PP
.B A refusal is a warning and a skip, never a failure,
for every event including one that permits a fatal hook. Aborting a shutdown or
an install because a plugin called back into it leaves the operator worse off
than the missing step does. The warning names the plugin, the event and the
chain, so what looped is readable from the journal.
.PP
A hook that has not exited within
.B 360 seconds
is killed, reaped and reported as failed. The bound is set by the stop path: the
unit's
.B TimeoutStopSec=380s
covers a worst case of 358 s, of which the elected console's
.B pre-stop
hook is 355 s once the 3-second election outside it is subtracted. The deadline
has to clear a legitimate countdown and still bound a hook that black-holes, so
that one costs an overrun rather than every second systemd has before it
SIGKILLs the JVM mid-chunk-flush.
.SH "[[providers]]"
One table per capability the plugin offers core, beyond a command and a hook.
.TP
.B kind
.RB ( source " | " console )
.B source
populates a staging directory from a file the operator names.
.B console
talks to a server that is already running.
.IP
An unrecognised kind is
.I reported and ignored,
not refused: the plugin's commands, hooks and understood providers still work.
A plugin built against a newer core must be able to contribute the parts this
core understands, or every future kind becomes a flag day for the whole plugin
set. The report is what keeps
.B kind = "sauce"
from being silently inert \(em
.B mc plugins
prints it.
.TP
.B name
The provider's name, shown by
.BR "mc plugins" .
.TP
.B extensions
.RI ( source )
File extensions claimed, without the dot.
.B mc install pack.mrpack
routes to whichever provider claims
.BR mrpack .
.TP
.B priority
.RI ( console ", integer, default " 0 )
Which console wins when more than one is installed. Higher is better. The
shipped consoles sit at 10
.RB ( mc-rcon )
and 20
.RB ( mc-mgmt ),
so a new one that should take precedence declares a larger number rather than
renumbering anything.
.SS "The source protocol"
Core invokes a source provider as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.RI < bin "> provide <" pack "> <" staging-dir ">"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The provider populates the staging directory and prints one JSON object on
standard output \(em
.BR server_type ,
.B minecraft_version
and
.B java_version
\(em with everything human-facing on standard error, so core can parse the
answer without stripping progress lines.
.SS "The console election"
Core does not choose a console by name. It asks each one whether it can talk to
.I this
server, right now:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.RI < bin "> console probe"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B The answer is the exit status and nothing else.
Exit 0 means usable. Anything else \(em a non-zero exit, a missing binary, a
protocol the server's version does not implement, an endpoint that is switched
off \(em means no, and core tries the next console down. A probe that has not
exited within
.B 3 seconds
is killed, reaped and counted as no. The bound is not decoration: election runs
on the shutdown path, and a black-holed endpoint must cost a couple of seconds
rather than the unit's whole
.BR TimeoutStopSec= .
.PP
The elected console is the highest-priority one that answers. It is resolved at
most once per process and only for the events that need it. A console that
prints on its probe puts a line in the journal every time the server stops, so
a console that is simply switched off says nothing.
.PP
.B "Only the elected console runs"
.BR pre-stop ", " pre-backup " and " post-backup .
Two consoles counting down announce a shutdown that already has a schedule, and
a second save-on can restore saving halfway through the other's archive. A
console that lost stands down for those events; a plugin that declares no
console provider is never suppressed.
.PP
.B "Every installed console runs"
.BR post-install " and " post-upgrade ,
elected or not, so each keeps its own credentials provisioned. Losing an
election must not leave a machine with nothing that works the day the winner's
probe starts failing.
.PP
.B mc plugins
names the outcome:
.PP
.RS
.nf
console:  mgmt (priority 20) \(em elected
console:  rcon (priority 10) \(em standing down
console:  rcon (priority 10) \(em not answering
.fi
.RE
.SH "WHAT MUST NOT MOVE INTO A PLUGIN"
The lock, the EULA gate, ownership of
.IR /opt/minecraft ,
and the system-managed keys of
.IR server.properties .
A plugin contributes a
.IR step ;
core keeps the ordering that makes the step safe. That is why a modpack
provider only fetches and stages: core re-applies the RCON keys afterwards, so
a pack cannot enable RCON with a password of its choosing.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS
abi  = 1
name = "hello"
bin  = "/usr/libexec/mc/mc\-hello"

[[commands]]
name  = "hello"
about = "Say hello"

[[hooks]]
event = "post\-install"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Installed as a
.B .deb
that depends on
.BR mc-server ,
alongside
.I /usr/share/man/man1/mc-hello.1
so that
.B mc man hello
has something to open.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Set by core on every invocation \(em command, hook, probe and source provider
alike. Paths travel here rather than being compiled into each plugin, so a test
can point core and its plugins at the same temporary root.
.TP
.B MC_ABI
The plugin interface version core implements.
.TP
.B MC_ROOT
The root every other path derives from.
.TP
.B MC_BASE
The server directory, normally
.IR /opt/minecraft .
.TP
.B MC_CONFIG
The configuration directory, normally
.IR /etc/minecraft .
.TP
.B MC_USER
The service account the server runs as.
.TP
.B MC_HOOK_DEPTH
How many levels of hook dispatch are already active. An integer; absent means
zero.
.TP
.B MC_HOOK_CHAIN
The
.IB plugin : event
pairs already active, comma-separated and outermost first \(em
.BR mgmt:pre-stop,backup:pre-backup .
.PP
.B The two hook variables are inherited, never reset.
Core passes them through unchanged to anything that is not itself a hook, and a
plugin must pass them on to anything it runs. Clearing them, or dropping them
while building a child's environment, hands the next process a fresh budget
simply for shelling out to
.BR mc ,
which is exactly the loop the guard exists to stop.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/
Manifests. Read in filename order, which is what makes hook order the same on
every machine.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/mc/
Plugin executables.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR mc-config (5),
.BR mc-rcon (1),
.BR mc-mgmt (1),
.BR mc-backup (1),
.BR mc-mrpack (1)
